<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bibi Khanym Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1399">1399</a>–<a href="/years/1404">1404</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bibi Khanym Mosque is a monumental congregational mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> between 1399 and 1404 after his Indian campaign and named after his chief wife. One of the largest mosques of its age, with a vast courtyard, towering portal and blue-tiled domes, it began to decay soon after completion because of its hurried construction. It has been extensively restored since the 1970s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After his Indian campaign in 1399 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> decided to build a gigantic mosque in his new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. He may have been inspired by the Friday Mosque at Sultaniyeh, built by Uljaytu. When he returned from a military campaign in 1404 the mosque was almost complete, but he was dissatisfied with the progress and had changes made at once, especially to the main cupola. Timur named the mosque after his wife Saray Mulk Khanum, although it was actually commissioned by her. It was meant to gather the entire male population of Samarkand for the joint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mosque is a courtyard mosque of the four-iwan type. Its outer walls enclose a rectangle 167 metres long and 109 metres wide. A parade portal about 35 metres high leads into the courtyard, opposite which rises a dome about 40 metres high over a square base. The three domes have a double shell, with a hollow space between the inner ceiling and the outer cupola. This let the interior above the mihrab keep a height of 30 metres while the outer dome was made as impressive as possible.</p><p>Open galleries 7.2 metres high once ran round the courtyard, roofed with small flat brick vaults and domes on more than 400 marble columns and buttresses. In the middle of the courtyard stands a huge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> stand of ornate marble blocks, which dates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s time. The interiors had gilding that imitated local brocade embroidery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosque had problems of structural integrity from the start. The scale of the plans pushed the building techniques of the time to their limit, and the haste of the work made it worse. After a few years the first bricks fell from the dome over the mihrab. In the late 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-khan-ii">Abdullah Khan II</a> cancelled all restoration, and the building slowly became a ruin, plundered for brick and marble. The inner arch of the portal collapsed in an earthquake in 1897. The government of the Uzbek SSR began a complex reconstruction in 1974, and the decoration of domes and facades was extensively restored. The mosque influenced architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bibi-Khanym Mosque” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Timur, Samarkand became one of the most lavishly built cities in the Islamic world, its monuments such as the <b>Bibi Khanym Mosque</b> paid for by the plunder of conquered lands; under Shah Rukh and Ulugh Beg, it became a real centre of intellectual and commercial life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
